From 131d668b247b3d8b6dc9b854d91a511b86e0d020 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work4_mq_assert <>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 09:11:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] os/kernel/pthread: add reproducer test for ASSERT in
 pthread_create()

os/kernel/pthread has exactly one always-compiled-in assertion; every other
one in that directory is a DEBUGASSERT and so exists only under
CONFIG_DEBUG.  The live one is in pthread_create():

  #ifdef CONFIG_APP_BINARY_SEPARATION
          ASSERT((sched_self()->flags & TCB_FLAG_TTYPE_MASK) != TCB_FLAG_TTYPE_KERNEL);
  #endif

Under app binary separation a pthread must belong to the task group of a
user binary, so pthread_create() rejects a call made on behalf of a kernel
thread.  It rejects it by panicking rather than by returning an error code,
although the following lines of the same function return EBUSY and ENOMEM
for other failures.

A user space task cannot reach the assert: every thread it can spawn is
TCB_FLAG_TTYPE_TASK or TCB_FLAG_TTYPE_PTHREAD, and only kernel_thread()
passes TCB_FLAG_TTYPE_KERNEL to thread_create().  The test therefore drives
it from inside the kernel through the OS API test driver:

  os/drivers/os_api_test/kernel/test_pthread_assert.c
    TESTIOC_PTHREAD_CREATE_FROM_KTHREAD spawns a kernel thread whose entry
    calls pthread_create()

  apps/examples/testcase/le_tc/kernel/tc_pthread_assert.c
    issues that ioctl on the test driver fd

Unlike the mqueue ASSERT(btcb), this one is not a race: the kernel thread
reaches pthread_create() and the assert fires on the first call, every time.

The test panics the board by design, so CONFIG_TC_KERNEL_PTHREAD_ASSERT
defaults to n and is not selected by CONFIG_TC_KN_ALL.  It depends on
APP_BINARY_SEPARATION because the assert does not exist without it; when the
assert is compiled out the test reports that rather than claiming a pass.
